<exercisegroup xml:id="exercisegroup-inverses-verify">
<title>Verifying inverses</title>

<introduction><p> In each case, determine whether the two matrices are inverses of each other. </p></introduction>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-verify-2x2-yes">

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 2 \amp 7 \\
				-1 \amp -4
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 4 \amp  7 \\
				-1 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Compute:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				A B \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 2 \amp 7 \\
					-1 \amp -4
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 4 \amp  7 \\
					-1 \amp -2
				\end{abmatrix}
				= \bidentmattwo
				\text{,}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				B A \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 4 \amp  7 \\
					-1 \amp -2
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rr}
					 2 \amp 7 \\
					-1 \amp -4
				\end{abmatrix}
				= \bidentmattwo
				\text{.}
			</mrow>

		</md>
		Since both <m>A B = I</m> and <m>B A = I</m> are true,
		these matrices are inverses of each other.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				-2 \amp 3 \\
				-1 \amp 2
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			C = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				 4 \amp 7 \\
				-1 \amp 0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		The matrix <m>A</m> is the same as in <xref ref="exercise-inverses-verify-2x2-yes" text="type-local" />,
		and that exercise confirms that <m>\inv{A} = B</m>
		(where <m>B</m> is also as in that exercise).
		Therefore, the matrix <m>C</m> in the present exercise <em>cannot</em> be inverse with <m>A</m>,
		according to <xref ref="theorem-inverses-unique" />.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rr}
				0 \amp  1 \\
				1 \amp -3
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				3 \amp 1 \amp 0 \\
				1 \amp 0 \amp 0 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p> These matrices cannot be inverses of each other because the are not the same size. </p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp  3 \amp 1 \\
				0 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\
				2 \amp  7 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				23 \amp -4 \amp -11 \\
				-6 \amp  1 \amp   3 \\
				-4 \amp  1 \amp   2
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Compute:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				A B \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					1 \amp  3 \amp 1 \\
					0 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\
					2 \amp  7 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					23 \amp -4 \amp -11 \\
					-6 \amp  1 \amp   3 \\
					-4 \amp  1 \amp   2
				\end{abmatrix}
				= \bidentmatthree
				\text{,}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				B A \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					23 \amp -4 \amp -11 \\
					-6 \amp  1 \amp   3 \\
					-4 \amp  1 \amp   2
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
					1 \amp  3 \amp 1 \\
					0 \amp -2 \amp 3 \\
					2 \amp  7 \amp 1
				\end{abmatrix}
				= \bidentmatthree
				\text{.}
			</mrow>

		</md>
		Since both <m>A B = I</m> and <m>B A = I</m> are true,
		these matrices are inverses of each other.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				2 \amp -4 \amp  7 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp  3 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp -3
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B = \begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				3 \amp -5 \amp 2 \\
				3 \amp -6 \amp 1 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Compute:
		<me>
			A B =
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				2 \amp -4 \amp  7 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp  3 \\
				0 \amp  1 \amp -3
			\end{abmatrix}
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				3 \amp -5 \amp 2 \\
				3 \amp -6 \amp 1 \\
				1 \amp -2 \amp 1
			\end{abmatrix}
			=
			\begin{abmatrix}{rrr}
				1 \amp 0 \amp  7 \\
				0 \amp 1 \amp  3 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp -2
			\end{abmatrix}
		</me>.
		Since <m>A B</m> does not evaluate to <m>I</m>,
		these matrices are not inverses of each other.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise xml:id="exercise-inverses-verify-4x4">

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle
			A = \begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
				0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
				1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>,
		<m>\displaystyle
			B = \begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
				 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
				-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
				 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
				 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
			\end{abmatrix}
		</m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Compute:
		<md>

			<mrow>
				A B \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
					-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
					 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
				\end{abmatrix}
				= \bidentmatfour
				\text{,}
			</mrow>

			<mrow>
				B A \amp =
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					 4 \amp -11 \amp 14 \amp -3 \\
					-1 \amp   3 \amp -4 \amp  1 \\
					 0 \amp   0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					 0 \amp   1 \amp  0 \amp  0
				\end{abmatrix}
				\begin{abmatrix}{rrrr}
					1 \amp 3 \amp -2 \amp  2 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  0 \amp  1 \\
					0 \amp 0 \amp  1 \amp  0 \\
					1 \amp 4 \amp  2 \amp -1
				\end{abmatrix}
				= \bidentmatfour
				\text{.}
			</mrow>

		</md>
		Since both <m>A B = I</m> and <m>B A = I</m> are true,
		these matrices are inverses of each other.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

<exercise>

	<statement><p>
		<m>\displaystyle A = \begin{bmatrix}      5      \end{bmatrix} </m>,
		<m>\displaystyle B = \begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{5} \end{bmatrix} </m>.
	</p></statement>

	<solution><p>
		Compute:
		<md><mrow>
			A B \amp =
			\begin{bmatrix}      5      \end{bmatrix}
			\begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{5} \end{bmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}      1      \end{bmatrix}
			\text{,}
			\amp
			B A \amp =
			\begin{bmatrix} \frac{1}{5} \end{bmatrix}
			\begin{bmatrix}      5      \end{bmatrix}
			=
			\begin{bmatrix}      1      \end{bmatrix}
			\text{.}
		</mrow></md>
		Since both <m>A B = I</m> and <m>B A = I</m> are true,
		these matrices are inverses of each other.
	</p></solution>

</exercise>

</exercisegroup>
